inline_comments.body:
Two problems with `get_device_module()` at the call site.

First, it resolves the **ambient accelerator**, not the device the timer belongs to. `sglang.srt.utils.common.get_device_module()` is `torch.get_device_module()` with no argument, which reads `torch._C._get_accelerator()`. On a CPU-only build that resolves to `torch.cpu`, which has no `Event`, so an `AttributeError` replaces the previous `torch.cuda.Event` construction. `ENABLE_METRICS_DEVICE_TIMER` gates the timer (`metrics_reporter.py:206`) so this is opt-in, but the PR's own goal is device-genericity, and CPU is a supported device -- worth either handling or stating as out of scope.

Second, it is resolved on every interval, twice per timed segment (`create` and `end`), on the forward path. Resolve it once at import or in `DeviceTimer.__init__` and store it, so the per-segment cost is an attribute load:

```python
@dataclass
class _TimingInterval:
    start_event: torch.Event
    ...
    @staticmethod
    def create(device_module):
        start_event = device_module.Event(enable_timing=True)
```

Minor: `torch.cuda.Event` does not subclass `torch.Event` (it derives from `torch._C._CudaEventBase`), so the new annotations on lines 89-90 are not a true supertype. Harmless at runtime, but `Any` would be more honest than a wrong hint.

